1. Character Count
Aim

To implement character count framing.

Theory
Counts the number of characters in the data.
Divides the data into frames of the given size.
Algorithm
Read the data string.
Read the frame size.
Count total characters.
Divide the data into frames.
Print the count and characters of each frame.
Program
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char data[100];
    int frame_size, current_pos = 0;
    int k, actual_frame_len;

    printf("Enter the data string: ");
    scanf("%s", data);

    printf("Enter the desired frame size: ");
    scanf("%d", &frame_size);

    printf("\nThe Total number of chars are : %d", strlen(data));
    printf("\nTransmitted Frames:\n");

    while (current_pos < strlen(data)) {
        if ((strlen(data) - current_pos) < frame_size)
            actual_frame_len = strlen(data) - current_pos;
        else
            actual_frame_len = frame_size;

        printf("%d ", actual_frame_len);

        for (k = 0; k < actual_frame_len; k++)
            printf("%c", data[current_pos + k]);

        printf("\n");
        current_pos += actual_frame_len;
    }

    return 0;
}
Output
Enter the data string: ACECOLLEGE
Enter the desired frame size: 4

The Total number of chars are : 10
Transmitted Frames:
4 ACEC
4 OLLE
2 GE

The manual also gives CSDDEPARTMENT, frame size 5 → 5 CSDDE, 5 PARTM, 3 ENT.

2. Byte Stuffing
Aim

To implement byte/character stuffing.

Theory
A special ESC character is inserted before a character that matches the FLAG or ESC.
F is used as FLAG and E as ESC in the manual.
Algorithm
Read the data.
Add starting FLAG.
Check each character.
If it is F or E, insert E before it.
Add ending FLAG.
Display stuffed data.
Program
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

#define FLAG 'F'
#define ESC 'E'

int main() {
    char data[100], stuffedData[200];
    int i, j = 0;

    printf("Enter the data string: ");
    scanf("%s", data);

    stuffedData[j++] = FLAG;

    for (i = 0; i < strlen(data); i++) {
        if (data[i] == FLAG || data[i] == ESC)
            stuffedData[j++] = ESC;

        stuffedData[j++] = data[i];
    }

    stuffedData[j++] = FLAG;
    stuffedData[j] = '\0';

    printf("Original Data: %s\n", data);
    printf("Stuffed Data: %s\n", stuffedData);

    return 0;
}
Output
Enter the data string: ACE
Original Data: ACE
Stuffed Data: FACEEF

Another manual example:

Enter the data string: CSDESC
Original Data: CSDESC
Stuffed Data: FCSDEESCF

3. Bit Stuffing
Aim

To implement bit stuffing.

Theory
Used to prevent data from being mistaken for a frame delimiter.
After five consecutive 1s, a 0 is inserted.
Algorithm
Read the binary data.
Count consecutive 1s.
Copy each bit to output.
After 5 consecutive 1s, insert 0.
Display stuffed data.
Program
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char data[100], stuffedData[200];
    int i, count = 0, j = 0;

    printf("Enter the data: ");
    scanf("%s", data);

    for (i = 0; i < strlen(data); i++) {
        if (data[i] == '1') {
            count++;
            stuffedData[j++] = data[i];
        } else {
            count = 0;
            stuffedData[j++] = data[i];
        }

        if (count == 5) {
            count = 0;
            stuffedData[j++] = '0';
        }
    }

    stuffedData[j] = '\0';

    printf("Data after bit stuffing: %s\n", stuffedData);

    return 0;
}
Output
Enter the data: 01111110
Data after bit stuffing: 011111010

Another manual example:

Enter the data: 011111111111111101
Data after bit stuffing: 011111011111011111001

⭐ Viva – Very Short
Question	Answer
What is framing?	Dividing data into frames.
What is character count?	Counting characters and dividing them into frames.
What is byte stuffing?	Adding ESC before FLAG/ESC characters.
What is bit stuffing?	Inserting 0 after five consecutive 1s.
What is FLAG?	A special character marking frame boundaries.
What is ESC?	Escape character used in byte stuffing.
Why is bit stuffing used?	To prevent data from being treated as control information.
